{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-fueltype-diesel-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the SOURCE TYPE dimension at its smallest discriminating width: the `expand-fueltype-diesel` run computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. It selects two source types, 21 (passenger car) and 32 (light commercial truck), and is otherwise `process-tirewear`'s scope with `expand-day`'s pollutant-processes.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES, which §23 asks for before a dimension is called covered.\n\n* THE SOURCE TYPE AS AN AXIS. Every other RunSpec in this tree selects one, so until now a model that summed `runspecsourcetype` into a scalar passed the whole suite -- and one did: `run_sourceTypeID` was exactly that sum. The drive cycle is the deep half. Vehicle-specific power is computed from the source type's own row of `sourceusetypephysics`, so VSP, the operating modes it bins into, the speed-bin brackets and the operating-mode distribution the rates are weighted by are all per source type, and a run at width two computes two of each.\n\n* A COHORT COUNT THAT IS A SUM AND NOT A PRODUCT. 248 rows, not 250: source type 21 carries 125 surviving (modelYearID, fuelTypeID) cohorts and source type 32 carries 123. Both select the same four fuels, so the difference is in `samplevehiclepopulation` -- two candidates have no sample vehicle and are not emitted. Nothing in the model declares 248. `runspecsourcefueltype` is the cohort grid's second factor and carries (sourceTypeID, fuelTypeID) PAIRS, `coh_rank` densifies whatever survives, and `cohortSurvivorCount` recomputes the total, so the ragged count arrives as one number that the shape check holds the emitted relation to.\n\nTHE TEN SELECTIONS, ONE BINDING AND THE ORACLE are everything this document carries. Against `runspecs/expand-day.esm` it differs in eleven `url_template`s and nothing else -- same binding, same structure -- which is the measurement PLAN.md §7.1 wanted: a second source type costs a per-fixture document nothing, because the cost was paid once in the domain model.\n\nWHY THIS DOCUMENT IS IN `runspecs/pending/` AND NOT `runspecs/`, as of 2026-09-18. It is complete and it validates; the model does not yet reproduce it, for ONE reason that is not the source-type axis. A MOVES source bin is (sourceTypeID, fuelTypeID, modelYearID, regClassID, engTechID) and this model's cohort candidate stops at the first three. Source type 21 has a single regulatory class (20) for every (model year, fuel) it carries, so the distinction was invisible for every run before this one; source type 32 has TWO for 333 of its 444 triples (30 and 41), 51 has up to four and 62 up to three. `coh_regClassID` is a `sum_product` over the matching `samplevehiclepopulation` rows, so on such a triple it returns 71 rather than 30 or 41, `emissionrate` matches nothing and the rate is 0.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY PROVEN BY RUNNING IT, which is why it is checked in rather than deleted: the emitted relation is the right SHAPE -- 248 rows, 125 under source type 21 and 123 under 32, from a declaration the chain recomputes -- and every one of source type 21's 125 rows reproduces `MOVESOutput` at worst cell 7.106e-06, the same worst cell `expand-day` reports. The axis works; the cohort is too coarse. Source type 32's 23 single-regulatory-class rows are wrong too, at 0.735, and they have the same cause one step back: their E85 activity is re-attributed from a gasoline cohort whose `coh_regClassID` is the 71, so `coh_usedFraction` finds no partner.\n\nWHAT IT WAITS ON: the cohort candidate grid gaining the regulatory class as a third factor, `coh_rank` ranking distinct (source type, fuel type, model year) TRIPLES rather than candidates, and the output relation's ordinal join summing the bins of a triple -- which the `faq` already does, so the output side is the small half. PLAN.md §7.3.5 has the measurement.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-day"
    ]
  },
